<section class="es-section" id="es-335">
  <p class="es-eyebrow">Model-by-Model Dating</p><h2>Gibson ES-335 Dating Timeline</h2>
  <p>The ES-335 starts in 1958 with two humbuckers, a rosewood fingerboard and simple trim. Its dot-to-block change and stopbar-to-trapeze change are useful dating clues, but neither gives an exact year by itself.</p>
  <article class="es-change"><p class="es-years">1958 to 1961</p><h3>Dot Neck, Long Guard And PAF Pickups</h3>
    <p>Early ES-335s have dot inlays and rounded cutaways, although the first 1958 body shape is a little narrower than the full “Mickey Mouse ear” outline that follows. Rounded horns dominate from later 1958 into 1963. The long pickguard extends below the bridge; the short guard begins appearing very late in 1960 and becomes established during 1961. Nickel hardware, PAF humbuckers and an ABR-1 with a stopbar are familiar features, with Bigsby-equipped examples also found.</p>
    <p>The 1958 and 1959 necks are generally fuller than the slim profiles that develop during 1960 and 1961. Neck depth varies between instruments. Very early 1958 guitars can have a shallow neck angle and a low-profile ABR-1. Many of those bridges later bent or were replaced, so bridge height isn't a one-year test.</p>
    <p class="es-meaning"><strong>Early ES-335 Dating Clue:</strong> A long guard, dots and a rounded body support an early date when the serial, FON and other original parts agree. Dots alone also fit later reissues.</p>
    <div class="es-photos"><ESPhoto src={roundHorn} id="335-dots-round-horn" title="Dots and Rounded Cutaways" alt="Dot inlays and a broad rounded cutaway on an early sunburst Gibson ES-335" caption="The round horn and dot-inlay board belong to the early ES-335 appearance. Later reproductions can copy both." /><ESPhoto src={longGuard} id="335-long-guard" title="Early Long Pickguard" alt="Long black ES-335 pickguard extending beside the ABR-1 bridge on a sunburst guitar" caption="Follow the pickguard’s lower edge beside the bridge pickup. On this 1959 example it extends farther toward the bridge than the shorter guard below." /></div>
    <div class="es-photos es-photos--single"><ESPhoto src={early335} id="335-early-controls" title="Early Bridge and Control Layout" alt="Sunburst ES-335 with nickel humbuckers, ABR-1 bridge, stopbar and amber bonnet knobs" caption="The ABR-1 bridge supports the strings; the separate stopbar anchors them. The amber bonnet knobs have no metal reflector inserts." /></div>
    <p>See the <a href="https://www.joesvintageguitarsaz.com/post/1959-gibson-es-335-authentication-guide/">1959 ES-335 authentication guide</a> for more close-ups of this guitar.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="es-change"><p class="es-years">1962 to 1964</p><h3>Small Blocks, Short Guard And Pickup Overlap</h3>
    <p>Small rectangular blocks replace the dots during 1962. Early 1962 dot-neck examples and later block-neck examples both exist. The shorter guard, nickel hardware and stopbar remain characteristic of this period. White nylon saddles appear during 1962 on the earlier no-wire ABR-1. The retaining-wire bridge appears in early 1963, with possible very late 1962 overlap. Bridges and saddles are easy to replace, so inspect the whole assembly.</p>
    <p>PAF and Patent Number pickups overlap in the early 1960s. A 1962 can have PAFs, and a transitional guitar can contain different decals. Neck profiles also evolve, with many 1964 examples fuller than the slim early-1960s necks. New body forms during 1963 bring a sharper horn outline, although leftover bodies can create later exceptions.</p>
    <p class="es-meaning"><strong>1962 to 1964 ES-335 Dating Clue:</strong> Small blocks place an original vintage guitar in the 1962-or-later group. They don't distinguish 1962 from 1964, or an old guitar from a modern block-neck reissue.</p>
    <div class="es-photos"><ESPhoto src={smallBlock} id="335-small-blocks" title="Small ES-335 Block Inlay" alt="Small rectangular pearl block in a rosewood ES-335 fingerboard" caption="This is the small block used on the ES-335, not the larger pearl blocks and ebony board of the ES-355." /><ESPhoto src={shortGuard} id="335-short-guard" title="Short Guard And Stopbar" alt="Cherry 1962 ES-335 with a short black pickguard, small block inlays and a stopbar" caption="This 1962 example combines small blocks, the short guard, a stopbar and black reflector knobs. Its no-wire ABR-1 has white nylon saddles, the bridge combination expected before the retaining wire becomes normal in 1963." /></div>
    <p>Compare the <a href="https://www.joesvintageguitarsaz.com/post/1962-gibson-es-335-guide/">1962 ES-335 guide</a> and the <a href="https://www.joesvintageguitarsaz.com/post/gibson-paf-patent-number-pickup-guide/">PAF and Patent Number pickup guide</a>. Keep original pickup covers soldered shut.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="es-change"><p class="es-years">1965 to 1968</p><h3>Trapeze Tailpiece, Narrower Nut And Chrome</h3>
    <p>Around 1965, the trapeze becomes the usual non-vibrato tailpiece and nickel gives way to chrome. The nut is generally about 1 11/16 inches through early 1965, about 1 5/8 inches during the transition, then about 1 9/16 inches from mid-1965 onward. Published 1968 specifications sometimes list a wider nut, but many observed guitars retain the narrow width. Measure the guitar instead of assuming a 1968 reset.</p>
    <p>Bigsby and other factory vibrato configurations complicate a simple stopbar-versus-trapeze rule. The short Maestro shown here is an unusual 1965 example. Inspect mounting holes, finish shadows and wiring before calling a different tailpiece a modification.</p>
    <p class="es-meaning"><strong>Later-1960s ES-335 Dating Clue:</strong> A narrow nut, small blocks and a trapeze fit the later 1960s, but an added stopbar doesn't make that guitar a pre-1965 model.</p>
    <div class="es-photos"><ESPhoto src={trapeze} id="335-trapeze" title="Later Trapeze Tailpiece" alt="Chrome trapeze tailpiece mounted at the end of a cherry 1966 Gibson ES-335" caption="The trapeze anchors at the body’s end instead of using two stopbar studs in the top. This example is from the 1966 ES-335 guide." /><ESPhoto src={shortMaestro} id="335-short-maestro" title="An Unusual Factory Vibrato" alt="Compact short Maestro Vibrola behind an ABR-1 with nylon saddles on a sunburst 1965 ES-335" caption="This 1965 ES-335 shows why the normal production pattern needs room for documented factory exceptions. The short unit has no long Lyre cover." /></div>
    <p>Witch-hat knobs begin appearing in late 1966 and are found on nearly all 1967 examples. Double-line Kluson housings and double-ring buttons are useful later-1960s comparisons. Patent Number pickups can include T-Top construction, but the decal alone doesn't reveal the bobbins under a sealed cover.</p>
    <div class="es-photos"><ESPhoto src={witchHats} id="335-witch-hats" title="Later Witch-Hat Knobs" alt="Black witch-hat volume and tone knobs with ribbed centers on a sunburst 1968 ES-335" caption="The overhead view clearly shows the ribbed cone and wide numbered skirt of the witch-hat profile. Lighting and wear can make a metal insert look gold or silver, so this photo can't establish its original color." /><ESPhoto src={klusons} id="335-double-line-klusons" title="Double-Line Kluson Tuners" alt="Kluson Deluxe tuners with two columns of lettering on a 1966 ES-335 headstock" caption="Read the housing stamp, button rings and mounting footprint together. These are the double-line, double-ring Klusons expected on an ES-335 from late 1964 through 1968." /></div>
    <p>Read the <a href="https://www.joesvintageguitarsaz.com/post/1965-gibson-es-335-short-maestro-vibrola/">1965 short Maestro example</a>, <a href="https://www.joesvintageguitarsaz.com/post/1966-gibson-es-335-authentication-guide/">1966 ES-335 authentication guide</a> and <a href="https://www.joesvintageguitarsaz.com/post/1968-gibson-es-335-guide/">1968 ES-335 guide</a> for individual instruments.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="es-change"><p class="es-years">Late 1960s Through the 1970s</p><h3>Neck Construction And Later Electronics</h3>
    <p>Laminated neck construction, a volute behind the nut and the Made in USA stamp become useful clues around the turn of the decade. They don’t all arrive on the same day. Later 1970s guitars can have maple necks and different pickup and switching arrangements.</p>
    <p class="es-meaning"><strong>1970s ES-335 Dating Clue:</strong> A block-neck guitar can belong to several decades. Check the actual neck construction and serial format; don't date it solely from the lack of dots. ES-335 Pro and other named variants need their own factory specifications.</p>
    <p>The Dot reissue appears in 1981, bringing the earlier dot-and-stopbar look back. Later Dot, Studio, Satin, figured-top and Historic versions can’t be dated by the vintage feature chart alone. See <a href="#serial-and-reissues">serial numbers and reissues</a> below.</p>
  </article>
</section>

<section class="es-section" id="es-345">
  <p class="es-eyebrow">Model-by-Model Dating</p><h2>Gibson ES-345 Dating Timeline</h2>
  <p>The ES-345 arrives in 1959 with split-parallelogram inlays, a rosewood fingerboard, a crown headstock inlay and gold hardware. Its vintage stereo Varitone circuit is a separate clue from its age. A converted 345 remains a 345.</p>
  <article class="es-change"><p class="es-years">1959 to 1961</p><h3>Gold Hardware, PAFs And Stereo Varitone</h3>
    <p>Early examples combine rounded horns, gold-covered PAFs and usually a stopbar or a factory vibrato. Long guards give way to shorter guards in the late-1960 transition, and the fuller 1959 neck generally becomes slimmer during the early 1960s. Gold reflector knobs replace bonnet knobs around 1960 on sunburst examples; cherry examples normally use black reflectors with gold inserts. Knobs are easy to replace, so color is supporting evidence only.</p>
    <p class="es-meaning"><strong>Early ES-345 Dating Clue:</strong> Split-parallelogram inlays identify the model, not the year. Unlike the 335, the vintage 345 has no dot-to-block transition to use as a date marker.</p>
    <div class="es-photos"><ESPhoto src={inlays345} id="345-split-parallelograms" title="ES-345 Split-Parallelogram Inlays" alt="Paired slanted split-parallelogram inlays on a bound rosewood ES-345 fingerboard" caption="This 1963 close-up shows the inlay pattern the ES-345 used from its introduction. It isn’t a feature added in 1963." /><ESPhoto src={controls345} id="345-stopbar-controls" title="Stopbar and Varitone Layout" alt="Sunburst ES-345 with gold hardware, reflector knobs, a stopbar and a six-position Varitone" caption="This 1963 example shows the established layout: four regular knobs plus the separate numbered Varitone selector." /></div>
  </article>
  <article class="es-change"><p class="es-years">1962 to 1964</p><h3>Gold PAF Overlap And Evolving Necks</h3>
    <p>The split-parallelograms and gold hardware continue while the neck profile, guard and body contours evolve. Gold-covered PAF stock can remain in use later on ES-345s than nickel-covered stock on ES-335s. A gold PAF on an early-to-mid-1960s 345 is therefore not an automatic contradiction.</p>
    <p class="es-meaning"><strong>PAF-Era ES-345 Dating Clue:</strong> Don't assign a 1950s date simply because a pickup has a PAF decal. Check both pickups, the original harness, serial and construction before narrowing the year.</p>
    <p>Use the <a href="https://www.joesvintageguitarsaz.com/post/gibson-paf-patent-number-pickup-guide/">PAF and Patent Number guide</a> for pickup identification. Wear through the gold can expose the nickel underplate over the nickel-silver base; that alone doesn't prove a chrome replacement.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="es-change"><p class="es-years">1965 Through the 1970s</p><h3>Narrower Nuts And Later Hardware, Still Gold</h3>
    <p>The narrower-nut transition around 1965 applies here too. A trapeze becomes the usual non-vibrato tailpiece around this period, while Bigsby-equipped guitars remain part of the model’s history. The ES-335’s nickel-to-chrome change is not a dating rule for a vintage gold-plated ES-345.</p>
    <div class="es-photos"><ESPhoto src={bigsby345} id="345-bigsby-varitone" title="Gold Bigsby and Varitone" alt="Gold Bigsby, numbered Varitone selector and black reflector knobs on a cherry 1966 ES-345" caption="A Bigsby can belong on a mid-1960s ES-345. Its presence does not force the guitar into the 1950s or establish that it was added later." /><ESPhoto src={pickup345} id="345-later-gold-hardware" title="Later Gold Hardware" alt="Gold humbucker cover and gold ABR-1 with white nylon saddles on a 1966 ES-345" caption="The model still uses gold plating in 1966. Nylon saddles and a pickup cover are supporting clues, not stand-alone dates." /></div>
    <p>Later-1960s examples can add witch-hat knobs, followed by changes in neck construction, headstock markings and pickup manufacture through the 1970s. A “Stereo” truss-rod cover or Varitone dial can remain after the electronics have been changed.</p>
    <p class="es-meaning"><strong>Later ES-345 Dating Clue:</strong> Read the neck, serial and original pot dates to narrow the production year. Read the circuit separately to establish whether the guitar still has its factory wiring.</p>
    <p>There are more photos in the <a href="https://www.joesvintageguitarsaz.com/post/gibson-es-345-history-value/">ES-345 history and identification guide</a> and the <a href="https://www.joesvintageguitarsaz.com/post/one-owner-1966-gibson-es-345/">one-owner 1966 ES-345 story</a>.</p>
  </article>
  <aside class="es-note"><h3>Modern ES-345s Can Be Different</h3><p>Some later Gibson ES-345s have mono wiring, no Varitone and nickel hardware. Those features can be factory correct on a modern version. Confirm its model and build year before applying the vintage gold-and-stereo specification.</p></aside>
</section>

<section class="es-section" id="es-355">
  <p class="es-eyebrow">Model-by-Model Dating</p><h2>Gibson ES-355 Dating Timeline</h2>
  <p>The ES-355 begins in late 1958 as the deluxe model, with ebony, large pearl blocks, multi-ply body and headstock binding, a split-diamond inlay and gold hardware. Its blocks are part of the original design, not evidence of the ES-335’s 1962 transition.</p>
  <article class="es-change"><p class="es-years">Late 1958 to 1959</p><h3>Large Blocks, Bigsby And The First Stereo Version</h3>
    <p>First-generation ES-355s commonly combine a cherry finish, full neck profile, gold PAFs, a Bigsby and gold Grover Rotomatic tuners. The stereo Varitone version joins the original mono model in 1959. A factory mono example has no Varitone selector hole.</p>
    <p class="es-meaning"><strong>Early ES-355 Dating Clue:</strong> A Bigsby and Grovers can be original equipment. Don't use the early ES-335’s usual Kluson-and-stopbar setup as a checklist for a 355.</p>
    <div class="es-photos"><ESPhoto src={head355} id="355-split-diamond" title="Bound Split-Diamond Headstock" alt="Multi-ply bound Gibson ES-355 headstock with a pearl split-diamond inlay" caption="The bound headstock and large split-diamond ornament identify the ES-355 family. They continue beyond the 1950s." /><ESPhoto src={blocks355} id="355-large-blocks" title="Ebony, Large Blocks and Deluxe Trim" alt="Large pearl blocks in an ebony ES-355 fingerboard beside multi-ply body binding" caption="The ES-355 uses larger blocks than the ES-335. This trim belongs to the model from the start." /></div>
    <div class="es-photos"><ESPhoto src={mono355} id="355-factory-mono" title="Factory Mono ES-355 Layout" alt="Cherry ES-355 with a Bigsby, four regular controls and no Varitone selector" caption="This 1959 mono example has no Varitone. Its black reflector knobs are a later style, so use the photo for the model and control layout, not as proof of original 1959 knobs." /><ESPhoto src={grovers} id="355-early-grovers" title="Early ES-355 Grover Tuners" alt="Gold Grover Rotomatic tuner housings on the back of a 1959 ES-355 headstock" caption="Gold Grovers fit this early ES-355. Later tuner changes need to be checked against the original holes and finish footprints." /></div>
    <p>See the <a href="https://www.joesvintageguitarsaz.com/post/1959-gibson-es-355-history-value/">1959 ES-355 guide</a> for the rest of this instrument.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="es-change"><p class="es-years">1960 to 1964</p><h3>Slimmer Necks And Changing Vibratos</h3>
    <p>The early-1960s neck profiles become slimmer, guards shorten and black reflector knobs with gold inserts become familiar on the cherry ES-355. The sideways Vibrola is documented as early as 1959 but is most associated with 1960 to 1962; the long Maestro with its engraved Lyre cover follows around 1963. Bigsby examples and factory variation mean the vibrato should support a date, not dictate one.</p>
    <p>Gold PAT PEND Grover Rotomatics remain normal through late 1963. Gold Kluson waffleback tuners then replace them and continue through the end of the original run. Gold PAFs can persist later than the nickel-covered versions on the ES-335. The mono and stereo question remains separate from the pickup decal.</p>
    <p class="es-meaning"><strong>1960s ES-355 Dating Clue:</strong> A Maestro is compatible with the model’s 1960s history. Check the actual mounting, model documentation, neck and original electronics before treating it as a replacement.</p>
    <div class="es-photos es-photos--single"><ESPhoto src={paf355} id="355-worn-gold-pafs" title="Worn Gold PAF Covers" alt="PAF humbucker covers on a 1959 ES-355 with much of the gold plating worn away" caption="The worn areas expose the nickel underplate over the nickel-silver cover base. Surface color alone can't distinguish a PAF from a later pickup." /></div>
    <p>Use the <a href="/gibson-tuner-dating-guide/">tuner chronology</a> and <a href="https://www.joesvintageguitarsaz.com/post/gibson-paf-patent-number-pickup-guide/">PAF and Patent Number pickup guide</a> for closer comparisons.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="es-change"><p class="es-years">1965 Through the 1970s</p><h3>Narrower Nuts And Later Neck Construction</h3>
    <p>The nut-width changes around 1965 also affect the ES-355. Later guitars can combine sharper cutaways, different knobs and tuner styles with laminated necks, a volute and Made in USA. Gold hardware, ebony and pearl blocks remain model clues rather than tight year markers.</p>
    <p class="es-meaning"><strong>Later ES-355 Dating Clue:</strong> Match the construction and electronics to the possible serial years. An original factory mono model and a stereo guitar converted to mono may play similarly but have different histories.</p>
    <p>For a documented conversion example, read the <a href="https://www.joesvintageguitarsaz.com/post/one-family-1959-gibson-es-355/">one-family ES-355 story</a>. Gibson introduced the separate B.B. King Lucille model in 1980; it isn't a late-1960s or 1970s ES-355 branch. Signature and Historic reissues also need their own model identification.</p>
  </article>
</section>

<section class="es-section" id="body-and-neck"><p class="es-eyebrow">Shared Clues, With Overlap</p><h2>Body Shape And Neck Measurements</h2>
  <p>The first 1958 bodies have narrower horns, followed by the full rounded “Mickey Mouse ear” shape from later 1958 into 1963. New body forms during 1963 bring sharper horns, although leftover bodies and later exceptions prevent a hard serial-number cutoff. Photograph the full front square-on, since camera angle can make the same cutaway look rounder or sharper.</p>
  <div class="es-photos"><ESPhoto src={roundHorn} id="shared-rounded-cutaway" title="Earlier Rounded Cutaway" alt="Broad rounded cutaway horn beside dot inlays on a sunburst ES-335" caption="The bulbous early outline is often called a Mickey Mouse ear. This ES-335 is a shape comparison for the family." /><ESPhoto src={pointedHorn} id="shared-pointed-cutaway" title="Later Pointed Cutaway" alt="More pointed cutaway horn beside small block inlays on a later ES-335" caption="The horn narrows and its tip becomes more pointed. Read the shape alongside the neck and serial, not as an exact date." /></div>
  <div class="es-checks"><article><h3>Measure The Nut</h3><p>Measure across the fingerboard at the nut in millimeters or decimal inches. The usual progression is about 1 11/16 inches through early 1965, roughly 1 5/8 inches during the early-to-mid-1965 transition and about 1 9/16 inches from mid-1965 onward. Dealer measurements vary, so don't round a borderline guitar into the wrong group.</p></article><article><h3>1968 Needs A Measurement</h3><p>Some period references list a wider nut again for 1968, but many observed examples remain about 1 9/16 inches. Treat a wider 1968 example as something to document, not a default rule. Nut width and neck depth are different measurements.</p></article><article><h3>Repairs Can Hide The Evidence</h3><p>A replacement neck, refinished headstock or repaired heel can obscure construction clues. The absence of a volute or Made in USA stamp doesn't prove a 1960s date, especially on reissues.</p></article></div>
  <p>See the <a href="/gibson-physical-features-hardware-guide/">physical features and hardware guide</a>, <a href="/gibson-headstock-logo-chronology/">headstock logo chronology</a> and <a href="/gibson-knob-dating-guide/">knob guide</a> for individual features.</p>
</section>
